## Backend Setup (Nest.js)

### 1. Dependencies
- To use WebSockets with Nest.js and Socket.io, you need to install two main packages:
  - `npm install @nestjs/websockets @nestjs/platform-socket.io`
- It's also recommended to install the development types for **`socket.io`**:
  - `npm install -D @types/socket.io`

### 2. The WebSocket Gateway
- In Nest.js, a WebSocket server is abstracted as a **`Gateway`**.
- A gateway is a class decorated with `@WebSocketGateway()`.
- This decorator tells Nest.js to treat the class as a WebSocket server.
> [!TIP]
> The `@WebSocketGateway()` decorator can accept an options object. You can specify a `port` if you want it to run on a different port than your main Nest.js application, or a `namespace` to logically separate different socket connections.

- To make the gateway active, you must add it to the `providers` array in your `app.module.ts`:
  ```typescript
  // app.module.ts
  providers: [AppService, ChatGateway],
  ```

### 3. Handling Events
- To listen for incoming messages from clients, create a method inside your gateway and decorate it with `@SubscribeMessage('event-name')`.
  - **`'event-name'`** must match the event name emitted by the client.
- To access the data payload of an incoming message, use the `@MessageBody()` decorator on a method parameter.
  ```typescript
  // chat.gateway.ts
  @SubscribeMessage('message')
  handleMessage(@MessageBody() data: string): void {
    // ... handle the message
  }
  ```
> [!NOTE]
> Alternatively, you can access the raw `client` and `data` objects by defining the handler as `handleMessage(client: any, data: any)`. However, using `@MessageBody()` is often cleaner if you only need the payload.

### 4. Broadcasting Messages
- To emit messages to all connected clients (broadcasting), you first need to get access to the server instance.
- Decorate a class property with `@WebSocketServer()` and Nest.js will inject the native server instance.
  ```typescript
  // chat.gateway.ts
  @WebSocketServer()
  server: Server;
  ```
- Use the `server.emit()` method to send an event to **all** connected clients, including the original sender.
  ```typescript
  // chat.gateway.ts
  @SubscribeMessage('message')
  handleMessage(@MessageBody() message: string): void {
    this.server.emit('message', message);
  }
  ```

## Client Setup (HTML & Socket.io)

### 1. HTML Structure
- The client-side requires the **Socket.io client library**, which can be included from a CDN.
- A basic UI consists of:
  - An element to display messages (e.g., a `<ul>` or `<div>`).
  - An `<input>` field for typing messages.
  - A `<button>` to trigger sending a message.

### 2. Client-Side Script
- **Connecting to the server**: Create a new socket instance by calling `io('http://localhost:3000')`, pointing to your Nest.js backend URL.
- **Sending messages**: Use `socket.emit('event-name', data)` to send a message to the server. The `'event-name'` must match the one used in the `@SubscribeMessage()` decorator on the backend.
  ```javascript
  // client-side code
  socket.emit('message', messageInput.value);
  ```
- **Receiving messages**: Use `socket.on('event-name', callback)` to listen for events broadcast from the server. The callback function will receive the data payload sent by the server.
  ```javascript
  // client-side code
  socket.on('message', (data) => {
    // Add the new message to the DOM
    appendMessageToChat(data);
  });
  ```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The event names used for `emit` on the client and `SubscribeMessage` on the server must match. Similarly, the event names for `emit` on the server and `on` on the client must match. In this example, the event name `message` is used for both directions.
